WHAT TO WRITE

analysis - two or three sentences on where this student stands. Use only the numbers above; never invent a figure, a percentage or an outcome that is not there. Name the outcomes they are weakest at and say plainly what is going wrong. If there are no practice records, say the plan starts from the syllabus instead and why that is fine.

day_themes - exactly {{ day_count }} short names, one per day in order, each saying what that day is about: "Resolving forces on a slope", "Projectile motion end to end". A day with a name reads as a lesson; a day that is only a list reads as a chore.

steps - one entry per step above, in the same order, with the same number of entries. Each has:
  - title: one line naming the real content, not the outcome code. "Work through projectile questions where the launch angle is given", never "Practise P-11-B-07". Under about 90 characters.
  - tasks: two to four concrete things to do in that sitting. This is the part the student actually follows, so it must be specific and countable:
      GOOD  "Attempt 8 MCQs where the angle is given and the range is asked"
      GOOD  "Write out the derivation of $R = \frac{v_i^2 \sin 2\theta}{g}$ twice from memory"
      GOOD  "Redo the two questions you get wrong, showing every step"
      BAD   "Practise questions"
      BAD   "Revise the topic"
      BAD   "Understand projectile motion"
    Name the actual physics, chemistry, biology or mathematics. Give a number wherever a number makes sense. Do not repeat the title as a task.

encouragement - one or two sentences. Honest and specific to what they are working on. No empty cheerleading, and never promise a grade.

RULES
- Do not add steps, remove steps, change a day, change a duration or change a target. The schedule is fixed; only the words are yours.
- Keep every task inside the minutes that step was given. Four tasks in a ten-minute sitting is not a plan a student can keep.
- A step marked "read" means understanding it first: tasks like "note the three conditions where the formula does not hold", not a test.
- A student marked "struggling" is getting most of these wrong. Be matter-of-fact, not alarming, and start them from the simplest case.
- An outcome marked "unknown" has never been practised. Do not call it a weakness - it has not been covered yet.
- Write maths in LaTeX between $...$, e.g. $v = u + at$, and chemical formulas with mhchem, e.g. $\ce{H2SO4}$. Write each LaTeX command with ONE backslash.
- Stay inside the FSc syllabus. Never send them to a resource that is not in the app.

